    "slug": "ethiopian-forces-exit-burhakaba-passing-security-to-somali-army",
    "summary": "Ethiopian troops under the African Union mission have left Burhakaba in Somalia's Bay region after nearly 12 years, transferring control to Somali forces amid a funding shortfall.",
    "content": "Ethiopian soldiers serving with the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM) have vacated Burhakaba, a town in the Bay region surrounded by Al-Shabaab militants. The withdrawal, which took place this week, hands security responsibility to the Somali National Army after roughly twelve years of Ethiopian presence in the area. AUSSOM confirmed the move in a statement, noting that the handover was facilitated with assistance from the UN Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS).\n\nBurhakaba has long been a strategic point in the fight against Al-Shabaab, with the militant group maintaining a presence in the surrounding countryside. The town's location makes it a key transit hub, and its security has been a persistent challenge for both Somali and international forces. The Ethiopian contingent had been stationed there since about 2013, providing a buffer against insurgent attacks.\n\nThe transfer comes at a critical juncture for AUSSOM, which is grappling with severe financial constraints. The mission, which succeeded AMISOM in 2022, has faced funding gaps that have hampered its operations. This withdrawal is part of a broader adjustment of peacekeeping forces in Somalia, as the African Union and UN work to streamline the mission's footprint while supporting Somali-led security.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the situation in Burhakaba and the broader AUSSOM crisis has direct implications. Many diaspora members have family and business ties in the Bay region, and instability there can affect remittances and plans for return. The handover to Somali forces raises questions about the capacity of the national army to hold ground without external support, especially in areas where Al-Shabaab remains active.\n\nThe development also underscores the delicate balance between Somalia's sovereignty and its reliance on international security assistance. As AUSSOM faces its most acute funding crisis, the effectiveness of peacekeeping operations in Somalia will be closely watched by diplomatic observers and the diaspora alike.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/ethiopia-troops-withdraw-burhakaba-somalia-aussom/",
